e as well we'll weave  through the crowds just trying to show  you a little bit of what this is all  about this is the Children and Youth  Pavilion and it was opened by Barbados  prime minister Mia Motley who's a bit of  a rising star in the climate crisis  movement it's become a bit of a hub for  activism and we've heard from a lot of  Youth activists voices today demanding  Justice and equity in particular arguing  that they are the future the boat is the  future the consumers of the future and  they demand change now there have been  big protests too of course about  environmental activists and their  freedoms to protest and there's been a  lot of concern about the number of  fossil fuel lobbyists who are here on  the ground of cop27 are reportedly some  600 more than all the island nation  delegates combined but all eyes really  looking forward now to the arrival of  United States of America President Joe  Biden he's going to give a speech here  can you reject some momentum into  proceedings is he going to talk about  loss and damage and about whether or not  America is willing to pay into a new  fund to compensate poor Nations for the  damage inflicted on them by climate  change is he going to start to talk  about coal and oil and gas and phasing  that out how will he bring the Diplomat  diplomatic might of America to bear at  this Summit because I think it's fair to  say it still is in need of some momentum  so that the people assembled here and  there's nearly 40 000 of them can have  real hope that 1.5 degrees of warming is  still very much with insight  and don't forget you can get all the  latest news from cop27 on our live blog  just scan the QR code that's on screen  now  well let's take a look now at some of  the other climate news of the day and  climate Justice activists have been  protesting at cop27 today demanding that  the world's biggest polluters are kicked  out of the conference more than 600  lobbyists for the fossil fuel industry  are registered to attend the summit  that's up more than a quarter on the  number of cop 26 and hundreds of people  have gathered to highlight the murdered  and jailed environment and human rights  Defenders whose names were read at the  biggest protest at the summit so far  meanwhile back in the UK climate  protesters from just stop oil have  caused disruption on the M25 for a  fourth day in a row two people were  arrested for causing a public nuisance  and another five suspected activists  were arrested in a car with climbing  equipment and banners  water and fizzy drinks are now free and  food is half price at the summit after  delegates struggled to find enough to  eat or drink in the first few days this  has been welcomed by delegates in Egypt  who have previously complained that the  limited food and drink was being sold at  New York and London prices some have  even said that the move could help to  make meaningful agreement at the summit  more likely  now it may not be quite as visibly  damaging to the planet as a coal burning  Factory but fashion is one of the most  polluting Industries on the planet  garment and Footwear manufacturing and  Supply accounts for up to 10 percent of  global carbon dioxide output human  exploitation is also a significant  Challenge in a sector where  International chains of production make  it difficult to find out how or even  where our clothes and shoes are being  made  in order to address growing consumer  concern about where their clothes are  coming from the United Nations economic  commission for Europe has launched a  project for an international initiative  to enhance transparency and traceability  in the industry  well joining me now from the extreme  hangout at the climate Summit is the  model actor and Ambassador for the  project Lily Cole so Lily Cole lovely to  have you on the show thanks so much for  joining us so explain to us why you feel  that transparency and traceability is  such an important issue for the fashion  industry  I think it's an important issue for  every industry  um we're looking at Fashion but we're  also looking at critical raw materials  agroforestry and the idea is that these  learnings could be applied to every  single industry it's important because  if we don't know what's going on we  can't make choices we can't make choices  as consumers there's a rising tide of  greenwashing happening because of the  lack of information that's available but  also companies can't necessarily make  the changes they need to make happen if  they don't understand their own Supply  chains which is often the case and also  governments won't be able to know  whether they are actually meeting the  targets they're setting because they  don't have the data and the information  to understand the impacts of the  businesses operating in their countries  and there has been way too much opacity  for too long it's not a technical  problem it's arguably a problem of Will  and political and corporate will to  bring the level of transparency that  needs to happen to supply chains that's  so interesting that you say that I mean  the fashion industry does have very  complex Supply chains you're saying that  there are some producers who aren't even  aware of what happening further down the  chain is that what you're saying  yeah  yeah I've consulted for fashion  companies that want to improve their  their impact and they genuinely can't  get the data to go back to the beginning  of the supply chain when they try to  they can often see the first layer or  two so the factories that are maybe  manufacturing the clothes but if you ask  them where does the cotton actually come  from who are the farmers what are the  impacts on the ground at the very  beginning of your supply chain they  often don't have access to that  information and if you imagine we live  in a world where it's been shown that  Google can Google's algorithms can  understand that a woman's pregnant  before she even knows she's pregnant  that's how well data can understand  customers I don't think it's a technical  problem it's a it's a it's a problem  where we focus our energy and we could  get this level of data and information  so that customers can make better  choices companies can make better  choices and ultimately governments can  do the job and and regulate and track  the goals that are being set  and we know that there are lots of  challenges in making sure that the  industry is has less of a carbon  footprint but is it particularly bad as  well because of the frenetic Pace at  which it moves the fact that the fashion  drives change constantly  yeah I think that's a very good question  and that's the other thing I've been  trying to talk about this week is not  just transparency it's also the rate of  production consumption and growth GDP is  this kind of religion by which most  companies if not all companies are being  led by as a primary metric and they need  to rethink other kpis to also think  about circularity the dress I'm wearing  I bought second hand you know how do we  expand and extend the lifespan of the  the products we already have in  circulation if you look at the level of  recycling in Fabrics the level of  recycling and electronics it's  shockingly low and we really really need  to reform that very quickly to reduce  the amount of extraction we're doing and  the amount of farming we're doing in the  natural world so yeah questioning  business models questioning the central  position of GDP growth is the key metric  and looking at circularity feel  essential to go anywhere near the  targets we're setting and on that point  I would just say you know the UN has  called for all Industries to halve  emissions 40 five percent reduction by  2030 to say within safe planetary  boundaries many fashion companies have  made the commitment to halve their  emissions by 2030 but if you look at the  data year on year emissions are still  going up around the world and so there's  this huge gap between the pledges that  are being made the promises that are  being made and the actual data of what's  happening and the reality is we can't  greenwash physics like physics is not  going to be greenwashed you know so we  have to be really truthful and really  realistic with ourselves as individuals  as communities as companies as countries  as an International Community to get  very realistic about making sure the  pledges we're making are being put into  practice and taking the probably quite  radical actions that need to happen in  order to put those Pleasures into  practice okay Lily Cole very interesting  to talk to us to talk to you rather  thanks very much indeed for joining us  there from charmel Sheikh thank you  and just before we go a quick reminder  that our weekly climate Show with Tom  Heap is back and you can watch it on  Saturdays and Sundays that's at 3 30 in  the afternoon or at 7 30 in the evening  and of course we'd love more questions  from you you can scan the QR code here  or you can go to any of our social media  accounts and put your questions in the  comments and Tom will answer some of  your questions on his show  but that's it for today do join us again  tomorrow at the same time for the very  latest on cop27 thanks for watching  foreign

